            'title' => 'Meat & Seafood',
            'question' => 'Can Cats Eat Meat and Fish?',
            'answer' => 'Yes, this is what a cat\'s body runs on. Keep it plain, cooked and boneless, since raw, bones and processed meats carry the real risk.',
            'meta_description' => 'Meat is what a cat\'s body runs on, but raw meat, cooked bones and too much liver each carry a real, checkable risk. See what\'s actually safe to feed.',
            'verdict' => 'safe',
            'note' => 'Cooked, plain and boneless',
            'image' => 'can-cats-eat-meat-seafood-chicken-fish',
            'alt' => 'Chicken, salmon and tuna beside a cat',
            'why' => 'Meat and fish are the one category on this site a cat\'s body is actually built to run on. Cats are obligate carnivores, a biological classification meaning they can\'t meet their basic nutrient needs from anything but animal tissue. The clearest example is taurine, an amino acid that keeps heart muscle contracting properly and the retina intact. Most mammals synthesize their own taurine from other amino acids, but cats can\'t, so it has to come from what they eat, and it\'s found almost exclusively in animal muscle and organ tissue, not in plants or grains. A cat fed a diet without enough taurine can develop dilated cardiomyopathy, a weakening and enlargement of the heart, and retinal degeneration, both well documented consequences of the deficiency. Complete commercial cat foods are formulated around this reality, built on animal protein with taurine present at the levels a cat needs. Plain cooked meat and fish sit close to that same foundation, which is why, unlike almost every other category on this site, the question here isn\'t whether cats should be eating meat, it\'s how it\'s prepared and which specific items or amounts cross into real risk. Raw meat and fish carry a genuine bacterial load, cooked bones turn brittle and splinter, organ meat fed too often overloads a cat with vitamin A, and a diet built heavily around fish brings its own separate problems. None of that changes the underlying picture: a cat\'s short digestive tract is built to process protein and fat efficiently rather than ferment plant fiber, its teeth are built for shearing rather than grinding, and its metabolism runs preferentially on protein and fat for energy rather than carbohydrate. Meat and fish are the default this page starts from as safe. The guidance below is about staying inside the boundaries that keep it that way, not about whether to feed it at all.',
            'guidance' => 'Plain cooked chicken, turkey, beef, lean pork or lamb, with all bones removed and nothing added, no salt, oil, butter, garlic or onion, is a solid addition to a normal diet, whether mixed into a meal or given as an occasional extra. As with any treat or topper, keep it under roughly 10 percent of daily calories so it doesn\'t crowd out a complete, balanced food; <a href="/tools/cat-calorie-calculator" class="font-semibold text-primary underline decoration-line-strong underline-offset-4">our calorie calculator</a> gives a working number for a specific cat\'s weight. Chicken is the protein most people reach for first, and <a href="/blog/can-cats-eat-chicken" class="font-semibold text-primary underline decoration-line-strong underline-offset-4">our chicken guide</a> covers plain cooked chicken in full, including skin, broth and seasoning specifics. Fish works the same way in small amounts, and <a href="/blog/can-cats-eat-tuna" class="font-semibold text-primary underline decoration-line-strong underline-offset-4">our tuna guide</a> goes deeper on canned tuna, portion size and why it should stay a treat rather than a diet staple. What tips plain meat and fish from a good addition into a real risk is almost always one of four things. Raw meat and fish carry salmonella and campylobacter, which can make a cat unwell and can also spread to the people preparing the food or cleaning a litter tray afterward; cooking removes that risk entirely. Bones, cooked ones especially, dry out and turn brittle in a way raw bone doesn\'t, so they splinter into sharp fragments that can catch in the throat or perforate the gut; raw bone is a more supervised, vet-guided topic and not a casual substitute. Organ meat like liver is genuinely nutritious in small amounts but concentrated in vitamin A, so it needs to stay occasional, not a regular feature. Fish specifically is best kept to a few times a week rather than a daily habit, since a fish-heavy diet carries its own separate risks around thiamine and fat balance, covered below. Processed meats, deli ham, bacon and sausage, are worth treating as a different food entirely, not a variation on plain meat, given their salt, preservatives and often onion or garlic seasoning.',
            'introduce' => 'Introduce a new meat or fish the same way you\'d introduce any new food: plain, cooked, in a genuinely small amount, one protein at a time. A teaspoon-sized piece of plain cooked chicken, turkey or white fish on its own, not mixed into a full meal, is enough for a first try, since the goal at this stage is watching how one specific food sits with a specific cat, not feeding a meal\'s worth of it. Offering two new proteins together, a bit of turkey and a bit of salmon the same day, makes it impossible to tell which one caused a problem if something goes wrong. After that first taste, the next 24 hours matter more than the first few minutes. Most reactions show up as vomiting, loose stool or reduced appetite within that window rather than immediately, so a cat that seems fine right after eating can still react later the same day. If nothing unusual turns up, that item can move into a normal rotation at the small, occasional amounts described above. If a cat does react, that\'s worth noting and avoiding going forward rather than retesting, since a genuine sensitivity to a protein doesn\'t typically resolve with repeated exposure. This same first-taste approach applies every time a new protein gets introduced, since a cat that handles chicken fine can still react differently to beef, pork or a new fish.',
            'items' => [
                ['name' => 'Plain cooked chicken', 'verdict' => 'safe', 'note' => 'Skinless, boneless and unseasoned, this is the protein most cats tolerate best and the one most complete cat foods are built around. See our chicken guide for full detail on skin, broth and bones.'],
                ['name' => 'Plain cooked turkey', 'verdict' => 'safe', 'note' => 'Lean and easy to digest cooked and unseasoned. Skip pre-brined or self-basting turkey, which is often injected with a sodium solution well beyond what a cat\'s kidneys need to process.'],
                ['name' => 'Plain cooked beef', 'verdict' => 'safe', 'note' => 'A good source of protein and iron in small, plain, cooked amounts. Trim visible fat first, since beef fat is calorie-dense and adds little a complete diet doesn\'t already provide.'],
                ['name' => 'Plain cooked pork', 'verdict' => 'safe', 'note' => 'Fine cooked through and plain, lean cuts only. Skip anything cured, smoked or seasoned, and always cook pork fully, since undercooked pork carries its own parasite risk beyond ordinary bacteria.'],
                ['name' => 'Plain cooked lamb', 'verdict' => 'safe', 'note' => 'A less common but perfectly reasonable protein cooked and plain. Naturally fattier than chicken or turkey, so trim visible fat and keep portions on the smaller side.'],
                ['name' => 'Plain cooked white fish (cod, haddock, tilapia)', 'verdict' => 'safe', 'note' => 'Low in fat and easy on digestion, cooked and boneless. A reasonable occasional addition, though like any fish it\'s better as a rotation item than a daily feature.'],
                ['name' => 'Salmon (cooked)', 'verdict' => 'safe', 'note' => 'Cooked, boneless and plain salmon is a good occasional addition. Oilier than white fish, so keep it a few times a week at most rather than daily, given the fat-balance issues fish-heavy diets can cause.'],
                ['name' => 'Shrimp (cooked, shelled)', 'verdict' => 'safe', 'note' => 'Plain, fully cooked and shelled shrimp, tail removed, is fine in small amounts. Skip fried, buttered or seasoned shrimp, and remove the shell and tail entirely, since they\'re a choking hazard.'],
                ['name' => 'Canned tuna', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Fine as an occasional small treat, not a regular feature. Tuna is one of the oily fish most linked to steatitis when overfed. See our tuna guide for portion specifics.'],
                ['name' => 'Raw meat', 'verdict' => 'unsafe', 'note' => 'Carries a real risk of salmonella and campylobacter that can sicken a cat and spread to people handling the food. Cornell Feline Health Center and the AVMA both advise against raw diets for this reason.'],
                ['name' => 'Raw fish', 'verdict' => 'unsafe', 'note' => 'Carries the same bacterial risk as raw meat, plus thiaminase, an enzyme in some raw fish that destroys vitamin B1 and can cause thiamine deficiency with regular feeding. Cooking eliminates both problems.'],
                ['name' => 'Liver and other organ meat', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Nutrient-dense but very high in vitamin A. Feeding it regularly over weeks to months can cause hypervitaminosis A, a buildup that leads to painful, irreversible bone changes. Keep it occasional and small.'],
                ['name' => 'Cooked bones', 'verdict' => 'unsafe', 'note' => 'Cooking dries bones out and makes them brittle, so they splinter into sharp fragments that can lodge in the throat or perforate the gut on the way through. Never safe, poultry bones included.'],
                ['name' => 'Raw bones', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Less likely to splinter than cooked bone, but still a real choking and tooth-fracture risk, plus the same salmonella and campylobacter exposure as any raw meat. Only worth feeding under a vet\'s guidance.'],
                ['name' => 'Deli or lunch meat and ham', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Usually high in sodium and often cured with nitrates, sometimes flavored with onion or garlic powder, which is genuinely toxic to cats. A plain, unseasoned sliver is lower risk than a regular habit.'],
                ['name' => 'Bacon and sausage', 'verdict' => 'unsafe', 'note' => 'Cured, salted and high in fat, and sausage in particular is frequently seasoned with onion or garlic powder. The combination of sodium, fat and hidden allium seasoning makes these worth avoiding as food, not just treats.'],
            ],
            'avoid' => [
                'Raw meat and raw fish: a real bacterial risk from salmonella and campylobacter that can sicken a cat and spread to anyone handling the food or a litter tray afterward.',
                'Cooked bones of any kind, poultry included: cooking makes them brittle enough to splinter and injure the throat or gut on the way through.',
                'Liver or other organ meat fed often or in large amounts: concentrated vitamin A builds up over weeks to months and can cause hypervitaminosis A, a painful, irreversible bone condition.',
                'Processed meats like deli ham, bacon and sausage: high sodium, nitrates, and often hidden onion or garlic powder. See <a href="/food-guides/toxic-foods" class="font-semibold text-primary underline decoration-line-strong underline-offset-4">our toxic foods guide</a> for what onion and garlic actually do.',
                'A diet built heavily around fish, day after day: raw fish brings thiamine breakdown, and too much oily fish without enough vitamin E is linked to steatitis, a painful fat inflammation.',
                'Fat trimmings, skin and cooking drippings offered regularly: a recognized pancreatitis risk factor, even though none of it is toxic on its own.',
                'Raw bones without a vet\'s guidance: less likely to splinter than cooked bone, but still a choking, tooth-fracture and bacterial risk.',
            ],
            'watch_for' => [
                'Vomiting or diarrhea within a day of raw meat or fish specifically.',
                'Drooling, pawing at the mouth, gagging or difficulty swallowing after any bone-in food, cooked or raw.',
                'Reduced appetite, a hunched posture or repeated vomiting after fatty trimmings or drippings, possible signs of pancreatitis.',
                'Stiffness, reluctance to move the neck, or painful joints developing over weeks to months on a liver-heavy diet, a sign of vitamin A toxicity.',
                'Lethargy, wobbliness, loss of appetite or vomiting developing over weeks on a raw, fish-heavy diet, a sign of thiamine deficiency.',
                'Tender or lumpy fat under the skin along with reduced appetite, signs of steatitis from too much oily fish.',
            ],
            'faq' => [
                ['q' => 'Can cats eat meat?', 'a' => 'Yes. Cats are obligate carnivores, meaning their bodies need nutrients, taurine especially, that come almost only from animal tissue. Plain cooked chicken, turkey, beef, pork or lamb, with no bones, salt or seasoning, is a genuinely good addition to a normal diet. The real risks in this category come from raw meat, bones and processed cuts, not from meat itself.'],
                ['q' => 'Can cats eat fish?', 'a' => 'Yes, in moderation. Plain cooked fish like salmon, white fish or a small amount of canned tuna is fine as an occasional addition. A diet built heavily around fish carries its own risks though: thiamine breakdown from raw fish, and steatitis, a painful fat inflammation, from too much oily fish without enough vitamin E.'],
                ['q' => 'Can cats eat raw meat or raw fish? Is raw meat safe for cats?', 'a' => 'Not recommended. Raw meat and raw fish carry a real risk of salmonella and campylobacter, which can make a cat sick and spread to people handling the food or a litter tray. Cornell Feline Health Center and the AVMA both advise against raw diets for this reason. Cooking removes the risk.'],
                ['q' => 'Can cats eat turkey, beef or pork?', 'a' => 'Yes, plain and cooked. Turkey, beef and lean pork, boneless and without salt, oil or seasoning, are all reasonable additions to a cat\'s diet in small amounts. Avoid fatty cuts, skin, and any pork that\'s cured or processed, like bacon or sausage, which carry a separate sodium and seasoning risk.'],
                ['q' => 'Can cats eat bones?', 'a' => 'Cooked bones should never be fed: cooking dries them out and makes them brittle, so they splinter and can injure the throat or gut. Raw bones are a more nuanced topic, less likely to splinter, but still a choking, tooth-fracture and bacterial risk, best handled only under a vet\'s guidance.'],
                ['q' => 'Can cats eat liver?', 'a' => 'In small, occasional amounts, yes. Liver is nutrient-dense but very high in vitamin A, and feeding it regularly over weeks to months can cause hypervitaminosis A, a toxic buildup that leads to painful, irreversible bone and joint changes. Treat liver as an occasional topper, not a regular protein source.'],
                ['q' => 'Can cats eat ham, bacon, sausage or deli meat?', 'a' => 'Best avoided as a regular food. Ham, bacon, sausage and deli meat are cured or processed with sodium and nitrates a cat\'s diet doesn\'t need, and often hidden onion or garlic powder, which is genuinely toxic to cats. An occasional plain, unseasoned sliver is lower risk than a habit.'],
                ['q' => 'Can cats eat salmon and shrimp?', 'a' => 'Yes, cooked and plain. Cooked, boneless salmon and shelled, cooked shrimp are both fine as an occasional addition, without seasoning, oil or butter. Keep portions small and infrequent rather than a daily habit, since a diet too heavy in fish carries its own separate risks.'],
            ],
            'deep_dives' => [
                ['label' => 'Can Cats Eat Chicken?', 'slug' => 'can-cats-eat-chicken'],
                ['label' => 'Can Cats Eat Tuna?', 'slug' => 'can-cats-eat-tuna'],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'slug' => 'dairy-and-eggs',
            'published_at' => '2026-08-17',
            'updated_at' => '2026-08-31',
            'title' => 'Dairy & Eggs',
            'question' => 'Can Cats Eat Dairy and Eggs?',
            'answer' => 'Cooked egg is fine in small amounts. Most adult cats lose the enzyme to digest lactose, so milk and cheese are occasional, not routine.',
            'meta_description' => 'Cats lose the lactase enzyme that digests milk after weaning, and raw eggs carry salmonella risk. See what\'s actually safe to feed.',
            'verdict' => 'caution',
            'note' => 'Eggs are fine, most dairy isn\'t',
            'image' => 'can-cats-eat-dairy-eggs-milk-cheese',
            'alt' => 'Eggs, milk and cheese beside a cat',
            'why' => 'Lactose intolerance in cats isn\'t an allergy or something that develops over time, it\'s a straightforward enzyme shortfall built into normal feline development. Nursing kittens produce plenty of lactase, the enzyme that breaks milk sugar down into digestible sugars, because milk is their entire diet. Once a kitten is weaned onto solid food, around 12 weeks old, the body has no more use for that enzyme and production drops off. By adulthood, Cornell Feline Health Center puts the number at roughly 65% of cats with meaningfully reduced lactase activity, enough to qualify as lactose intolerant. In one of those cats, undigested lactose passes into the large intestine, where gut bacteria ferment it, producing gas, and where it also pulls water into the bowel by osmosis, the same basic mechanism behind most diarrhea. That combination is why a saucer of milk so reliably produces loose stool within about 8 to 12 hours, not immediately and not days later. It isn\'t dangerous the way onion or chocolate is, just a predictable, avoidable stomach upset. Eggs run on a different mechanism entirely, and cooked, they\'re a genuinely better story. Egg is a complete, highly digestible protein with an amino acid profile well suited to a cat\'s biology, closer to what a cat is built to eat than dairy is. The real risk with eggs isn\'t a built-in digestive limitation, it\'s specific to raw egg: bacterial contamination, salmonella in particular, and a protein in raw egg white called avidin, which binds the B vitamin biotin and blocks its absorption. Cooking neutralizes both problems at once, heat kills the bacteria and denatures the avidin protein, so a properly cooked egg carries neither risk. That\'s the split this whole category comes down to: dairy is a mixed bag because of how a normal adult cat\'s gut works, eggs are fine specifically because they\'re cooked.',
            'guidance' => 'Treat plain cooked egg, scrambled or hard-boiled with nothing added, no salt, butter or seasoning, as a legitimate small addition rather than an occasional novelty. A tablespoon or two of chopped cooked egg a couple of times a week is reasonable for an average adult cat, well within the roughly 10% of daily calories that treats and extras should stay under; use <a href="/tools/cat-calorie-calculator" class="font-semibold text-primary underline decoration-line-strong underline-offset-4">our calorie calculator</a> to check where that lands for a specific cat\'s weight, and see <a href="/blog/can-cats-eat-eggs" class="font-semibold text-primary underline decoration-line-strong underline-offset-4">our eggs guide</a> for more on eggs specifically. Egg yolk carries more fat and calories than the white, so it should count toward that same treat budget, not sit on top of a full egg serving. Dairy deserves a more cautious default. Milk and cream carry the most lactose per serving and cause the most predictable upset, so they\'re worth skipping for any cat with unknown tolerance, and worth dropping entirely once a cat has reacted to either. Hard, aged cheeses like cheddar or parmesan are a different case: most of the lactose in milk is removed with the whey during cheesemaking, so these cheeses carry only a trace of it, which is why some cats tolerate a pea-sized cube where they couldn\'t tolerate milk; see <a href="/blog/can-cats-eat-cheese" class="font-semibold text-primary underline decoration-line-strong underline-offset-4">our cheese guide</a> for the full picture. Plain yogurt sits in a similar middle ground, since its live cultures consume some lactose during fermentation, so a small spoonful is tolerated by some cats that react to milk. Cottage cheese and soft cheeses are fresher and less fermented, so they keep more of their original lactose and behave closer to milk than to hard cheese. None of this is a reason to build dairy into a routine: even a well-tolerated cheese or yogurt is best kept to an occasional, teaspoon-scale taste, not a daily habit, since the fat also adds calories a cat\'s diet doesn\'t need on top of a complete food.',
            'introduce' => 'When trying any dairy or egg item for the first time, start with a genuinely small amount, a pea-sized piece of cooked egg or a half-teaspoon of plain yogurt or hard cheese, not a full serving, and offer only one new item at a time. Introducing several new foods together makes it impossible to tell which one caused a problem if a cat\'s stomach reacts. After that first taste, watch the next 24 hours rather than just the first few minutes: lactose-related diarrhea from dairy typically shows up within 8 to 12 hours, not immediately, so a cat that seems fine right after eating can still develop loose stool later the same day. If nothing unusual shows up, small, occasional amounts along the lines described above are reasonable going forward. If loose stool, gas or vomiting does appear, that\'s a reasonable item to drop from that cat\'s diet rather than testing it again to see if the first reaction was a fluke, since lactase levels don\'t recover with repeated exposure. The same small-amount-first approach is worth applying to a new cheese, milk or yogurt brand too, since fat content and any added ingredients can vary even when the base food looks the same.',
            'items' => [
                ['name' => 'Whole milk', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Cow\'s milk carries the highest lactose load of any common dairy item, and roughly 65% of adult cats lack enough lactase to digest it, so a saucer routinely causes gas and diarrhea within 8 to 12 hours.'],
                ['name' => 'Cream', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Higher in fat than milk but still meaningfully lactose-rich, so it carries the same GI-upset risk milk does, plus its extra fat and calories make a regular habit of it worth avoiding even in tolerant cats.'],
                ['name' => 'Hard cheese (cheddar, parmesan, swiss)', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Around 90% of milk\'s lactose is removed with the whey during hard-cheese making, leaving these close to lactose-free. A pea-sized cube is the dairy item cats most often tolerate without loose stool.'],
                ['name' => 'Soft cheese (brie, cream cheese)', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Not aged or pressed the way hard cheese is, so soft cheese keeps more of its original lactose and fat. It behaves closer to milk than to cheddar in how likely it is to upset a sensitive cat.'],
                ['name' => 'Plain yogurt', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Live cultures consume some lactose during fermentation, which is why lactose-intolerant cats sometimes tolerate a small spoonful of plain yogurt better than milk. Always skip flavored or sweetened varieties, plain and unsweetened only.'],
                ['name' => 'Cottage cheese', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'A fresh cheese, not aged or pressed, so it keeps roughly 2 to 4 grams of lactose per serving, meaningfully more than hard cheese. Treat it like milk, an occasional taste, not a regular topper.'],
                ['name' => 'Cooked egg (whole)', 'verdict' => 'safe', 'note' => 'A complete, highly digestible protein cats handle well. Cooking, scrambled or hard-boiled with nothing added, kills any bacteria and neutralizes avidin, the raw-white protein that otherwise blocks biotin absorption. A genuinely good small addition.'],
                ['name' => 'Raw egg', 'verdict' => 'unsafe', 'note' => 'Carries a real salmonella and campylobacter risk, and raw egg white contains avidin, which binds biotin and blocks its absorption. Cooking eliminates both problems, so there\'s no upside to feeding it raw over cooked.'],
                ['name' => 'Egg yolk (cooked, alone)', 'verdict' => 'safe', 'note' => 'Fine cooked, and actually a good natural source of biotin, but it carries most of an egg\'s fat and calories. Count it toward the same small treat portion as a whole egg, not on top of it.'],
                ['name' => 'Butter', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Almost pure milkfat, so it carries only trace lactose, but a regular lick adds calories a cat\'s diet doesn\'t need and can contribute to pancreatitis risk over time. Not toxic, just nutritionally pointless.'],
                ['name' => 'Sour cream', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Fermented like yogurt but carries more fat and still meaningful lactose. A small lick off a spoon is low-risk as a one-off; treating it as a regular topper risks the same loose stool milk causes.'],
                ['name' => 'Goat milk', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Often marketed as easier to digest, but its lactose content is close to cow\'s milk, only slightly lower, not a meaningful difference for a lactose-intolerant cat. It causes much the same GI upset milk does.'],
                ['name' => 'Lactose-free cat milk products', 'verdict' => 'safe', 'note' => 'Commercial cat-milk treats are formulated without lactose, or with lactase added, so they sidestep the digestive issue entirely. See our treats and snacks guide for more on treat-specific products like these.'],
            ],
            'avoid' => [
                'Raw eggs in any form, including raw yolk mixed into food: a real salmonella and campylobacter risk, plus the avidin protein in raw white that blocks biotin absorption. Cook eggs through before offering them.',
                'Ice cream, flavored yogurt, and anything with added sugar or chocolate: these stack a dairy lactose problem on top of a separate sugar or chocolate risk. See <a href="/food-guides/sweets" class="font-semibold text-primary underline decoration-line-strong underline-offset-4">our sweets guide</a> for what makes those specifically dangerous.',
                'Milk or cream offered as a daily habit rather than an occasional taste, since that pattern is the one most likely to produce chronic loose stool in a lactose-intolerant cat.',
                'Seasoned, salted or buttered eggs and cheese meant for a human plate, garlic and onion powder especially, which carry their own separate toxicity entirely apart from the dairy or egg itself.',
                'Any dairy for a cat with a known history of loose stool after milk or cheese: a past reaction is a reliable predictor, not a fluke worth retesting.',
