            ],
            'faq' => [
                ['q' => 'Can cats drink milk?', 'a' => 'Not safely as a regular habit. Roughly 65% of adult cats lose most of the lactase enzyme needed to digest milk sugar after weaning, per Cornell Feline Health Center, so a saucer of milk typically causes gas and diarrhea within 8 to 12 hours. Healthy adult cats have no nutritional need for milk at all, so skip it as a default, not just when it upsets a particular cat.'],
                ['q' => 'Can cats eat cheese?', 'a' => 'In small amounts, sometimes. Hard, aged cheeses like cheddar or parmesan lose most of their lactose during cheesemaking, so a pea-sized cube is the dairy item cats most often tolerate. Soft or fresh cheeses, cottage cheese and cream cheese included, keep more lactose and behave more like milk.'],
                ['q' => 'Can cats have yogurt?', 'a' => 'A small spoonful of plain, unsweetened yogurt is reasonable for many cats. Fermentation consumes some of the lactose during yogurt-making, which is why lactose-intolerant cats sometimes tolerate it better than milk. Always skip flavored, sweetened or chocolate-swirled varieties, and start with a half-teaspoon to see how an individual cat\'s stomach reacts.'],
                ['q' => 'Can cats eat raw eggs?', 'a' => 'No. Raw eggs carry a real risk of salmonella and campylobacter contamination, and raw egg white contains avidin, a protein that binds biotin and blocks its absorption. Cooking eliminates both problems at once, heat kills the bacteria and denatures avidin, so there\'s no upside to raw over cooked. Serve eggs scrambled or hard-boiled instead.'],
                ['q' => 'Why are cats lactose intolerant?', 'a' => 'Kittens make plenty of lactase, the enzyme that digests milk sugar, because they nurse. Once weaned, around 12 weeks old, that production drops off since it\'s no longer needed, and most cats never regain it. Without lactase, undigested lactose ferments in the gut and draws in water, producing the gas and diarrhea people associate with cats and milk.'],
                ['q' => 'Can cats eat butter?', 'a' => 'A lick isn\'t dangerous, but there\'s no reason to offer it on purpose. Butter is nearly pure milkfat with only trace lactose, so it won\'t typically trigger the diarrhea milk does, but it adds calories a cat\'s diet doesn\'t need and, as a habit, can contribute to pancreatitis and weight gain over time.'],
                ['q' => 'Can cats eat sour cream?', 'a' => 'A small lick as a one-off is low-risk for most cats. Sour cream is fermented like yogurt but carries more fat and still meaningful lactose, so treating it as a regular topper risks the same loose stool a lactose-intolerant cat gets from milk. Keep it occasional, not routine.'],
                ['q' => 'Can cats eat ice cream?', 'a' => 'Not a good idea beyond an accidental lick. Ice cream stacks a lactose problem on top of added sugar, and some flavors, chocolate and coffee especially, contain genuine toxins on top of that. See <a href="/food-guides/sweets" class="font-semibold text-primary underline decoration-line-strong underline-offset-4">our sweets guide</a> for which flavors are actually dangerous rather than just an upset stomach.'],
            ],
            'deep_dives' => [
