            'note' => 'Under 10% of daily calories',
            'image' => 'can-cats-eat-cat-treats-snacks',
            'alt' => 'Cat treats in a white bowl beside a cat',
            'why' => 'Treats are how most day to day training and bonding with a cat actually happens. A reward timed to a behavior within a second or two teaches a cat to associate that behavior with something good, which is the entire mechanism behind clicker training, carrier training, or just teaching a cat to come when called. None of that requires much food. A pea-sized piece delivered at the right moment does the behavioral job just as well as a handful, since the timing and consistency of a reward matter more than its size. The real risk in this category was never treats existing at all, it is scale and quality. A cat\'s total daily calorie need is small to begin with, often only 200 to 250 calories for an average indoor cat, so treats handed out across a few training sessions, a greeting at the door, and a bedtime routine add up faster than they look, especially since treats rarely get weighed or counted toward a meal the way regular food does. That is calorie creep: no single treat is the problem, the running total across a day is. Quality compounds that risk rather than causing a separate one. A treat built mostly from corn syrup, glycerin, and artificial coloring, with a trace of actual meat, delivers calories without much nutritional payoff, so a cat can sit right at its daily treat ceiling while getting comparatively little from it. That is a real reason to glance at an ingredient label, not a reason to treat every packaged treat as suspect. A genuinely short, meat-forward ingredient list is common on the market and worth choosing over a filler-heavy one, and a plain reading of the package is usually enough to tell the difference between the two.',
            'guidance' => 'The guideline used across pet nutrition sources is to keep treats of any kind under about 10 percent of a cat\'s total daily calories, with the other 90 percent coming from a complete, balanced food formulated to meet every actual nutrient requirement, taurine included. For an average indoor cat eating somewhere around 200 to 250 calories a day, that ceiling works out to roughly 20 to 25 calories, a small number in practice: most single commercial treats run about 2 to 3 calories apiece, so that is somewhere around 7 to 10 treats spread across a day, not a whole handful at once. A cat on a weight-loss plan or with a smaller calorie budget has even less room, which is exactly why a flat treat count matters less than a percentage of that specific cat\'s total. Commercial cat treats are, as a category, formulated with that math already in mind. Reputable brands publish a calorie count per treat or per gram on the bag specifically so that math is possible, and the ingredients in a mainstream treat are chosen to be safe for a cat\'s body at the portion sizes actually printed on the label, not just palatable. That is a different design goal from a human snack, which is formulated for a person\'s taste and calorie needs with zero consideration for a cat at all. Plain, unseasoned human food, a small piece of cooked chicken or a bite of cooked egg, still works within the same 10 percent ceiling, since the concern there is seasoning and portion size, not the base ingredient itself. Getting an exact number for a specific cat, rather than working from the 200 to 250 calorie average used above, is worth doing before treats become a daily habit. Our <a href="/tools/cat-calorie-calculator" class="font-semibold text-primary underline decoration-line-strong underline-offset-4">cat calorie calculator</a> works out a real daily calorie total from a cat\'s weight, life stage, and activity level, which turns the 10 percent rule from a rough guess into an actual number of calories to budget for treats each day.',
            'introduce' => 'A new treat or snack, whatever it is, deserves the same cautious first try as a new food: a small amount, not a full serving, and nothing else new offered alongside it. A single piece, or a piece cut down to about a quarter of what the package suggests for a first try, is enough to see how a cat\'s stomach handles it without risking much if it does not agree with them. For a homemade human-food treat like a piece of plain chicken or a taste of cooked egg, the same logic applies: pea-sized for a first offering, not a spoonful. Wait roughly 24 hours before introducing anything else new, watching for vomiting, diarrhea, itching, or a cat that goes off its regular food, any of which is a reason to stop and not offer that specific treat again. One new item at a time is what makes a reaction traceable: a cat given three unfamiliar treats in the same afternoon and then vomiting gives no way to know which one caused it, while a single new item and a clean 24-hour window turns a bad reaction into useful information instead of a guessing game. A cat that shows no interest in a new treat at all is simply not a fan of that one, not a sign anything is wrong, cats are individually picky about texture and flavor the same way people are.',
            'items' => [
                ['name' => 'Cat grass', 'verdict' => 'safe', 'note' => 'Real grass, not toxic houseplants, grown from wheat, oat, barley, or rye seed sold for pets. May help move swallowed hair along and adds trace vitamins. Overwatering grows mold, and outdoor lawn grass can carry pesticide residue or parasite eggs.'],
