                ['name' => 'Cooked egg (plain)', 'verdict' => 'safe', 'note' => 'Fully cooked, scrambled or boiled, no salt, butter, milk, or seasoning. A good small source of protein. Favor the white over the yolk, which is fat-dense enough to matter for a cat prone to pancreatitis or already managing its weight.'],
                ['name' => 'Commercial cat treats (bagged or pouched)', 'verdict' => 'safe', 'note' => 'Mainstream bagged and pouched treats are formulated to be safe at the printed portion size. Check the calorie count rather than trusting a "light" or "low-calorie" claim, often not meaningfully lower than the regular version.'],
                ['name' => 'Freeze-dried meat treats (chicken or salmon)', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Freeze-drying reduces bacteria in raw meat, it does not eliminate it, so Salmonella and Listeria exposure is a real, documented risk. Favor a single-ingredient product from a brand stating a validated pathogen-kill step like HPP, and wash hands after handling.'],
                ['name' => 'Dental treats and dental chews', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Mechanically scrub plaque as a cat chews, but that benefit is proven only for products carrying the Veterinary Oral Health Council seal. An unproven or overly hard chew mainly adds calories and a real tooth-fracture risk without doing the job.'],
                ['name' => 'Catnip treats', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'The nepetalactone response itself is harmless and non-addictive per VCA. Skip it for a cat with a seizure history, since it may lower seizure threshold, and check the label: many packaged "catnip" treats are seasoned chicken-meal snacks, not plain herb.'],
                ['name' => 'Plain cooked fish', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Boneless, plain-cooked, no oil or seasoning, as an occasional bite rather than a routine one. Repeated fish-heavy feeding is linked to steatitis and to taurine-deficiency heart disease, since canning leaches out taurine. Favor lower-mercury species over tuna or swordfish.'],
                ['name' => 'Cheese (small amount)', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'A dice-sized piece of plain, aged cheese like cheddar, once or twice a week. Most adult cats have reduced lactase and react with GI upset. Skip moldy cheese, a tremor-linked toxin risk, or anything flavored with onion or garlic.'],
                ['name' => 'Canned tuna (small amount)', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Light tuna in water only, capped around a tablespoon, no more than once a week. Never albacore, which carries roughly three times the mercury. Repeated feeding risks vitamin-E-deficiency steatitis, since tuna is not supplemented the way a complete diet is.'],
                ['name' => 'Lactose-free "cat milk" treats', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Labeled lactose-free products are typically lactose-reduced to well under one percent, not truly zero, and still carry added sugar and fat. A teaspoon once or twice a week is plenty. Never substitute kitten milk replacer, a different, complete-nutrition product entirely.'],
                ['name' => 'Plain air-popped popcorn', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => "A piece or two of fully popped, plain, unbuttered popcorn is low-risk for a healthy adult cat. Kernels are a real choking hazard for a cat's mouth, and buttered or salted popcorn adds fat and sodium cats do not need."],
                ['name' => 'Pretzels (plain, unsalted)', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'A plain, unsalted pretzel is mostly a zero-nutrition novelty, not an acute poisoning risk in a small piece. Garlic, onion, "everything," or ranch-seasoned pretzels are the real hazard here, a common grocery-aisle flavor carrying concentrated Allium seasoning.'],
                ['name' => 'Baby food meat puree', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Plain, single-ingredient infant meat purees, no onion or garlic on the label, are sometimes used short-term to tempt a sick cat to eat. Skip mixed vegetable or grain "dinner" varieties, which inconsistently contain onion powder even under the same brand.'],
                ['name' => 'Deli or lunch meat', 'verdict' => 'caution', 'note' => 'Sodium load and, more seriously, onion or garlic powder folded into a generic "seasoning" label are the real risks, since US rules do not require naming Allium powder individually. A truly plain, unseasoned slice is rare and still sodium-heavy.'],
                ['name' => 'Beef jerky and human jerky-style snacks', 'verdict' => 'unsafe', 'note' => 'Onion and garlic powder are standard seasoning in virtually all commercial beef and human jerky, a confirmed cause of hemolytic anemia in cats. Our toxic foods guide covers the full mechanism. Never offer human jerky, plain freeze-dried meat instead.'],
