            'faq' => [
                ['q' => 'Can cats eat treats?', 'a' => 'Yes. Commercial cat treats and plain, unseasoned human snacks like a bite of cooked chicken are fine in normal amounts. The real risk is not treats existing as a category, it is letting them push past roughly 10 percent of daily calories, or picking one hiding onion, garlic, or heavy seasoning.'],
                ['q' => 'How many treats can a cat have a day?', 'a' => 'Enough to stay under about 10 percent of daily calories, not a fixed number, since treats vary widely in calorie count. For an average indoor cat eating 200 to 250 calories a day, that works out to roughly 20 to 25 calories, and our calorie calculator turns that into an exact figure for a specific cat\'s weight and activity level.'],
                ['q' => 'Are cat treats bad for cats?', 'a' => 'Not inherently. Commercial treats are formulated to be safe at the portion sizes printed on the label, and the actual failure mode is overfeeding them or choosing a filler-heavy product, not treats as a category. Real problems trace back to calorie creep, hidden onion or garlic seasoning, or excess sodium, not to the idea of treats itself.'],
                ['q' => 'What human snacks can cats eat?', 'a' => 'Plain cooked chicken or fish, a small bite of cooked egg, or a dice-sized piece of plain cheese all count as reasonable human snacks for a cat, kept to a small amount. Skip anything seasoned, salted, sugared, or containing onion or garlic powder, all common in the real-world versions of these foods people actually eat.'],
                ['q' => 'Are dental treats good for cats?', 'a' => 'Only ones carrying the Veterinary Oral Health Council seal, which certifies that a product\'s plaque and tartar-control claim actually held up in testing. An unproven or overly hard dental chew mainly adds calories and a real tooth-fracture risk in an aggressive chewer, without reliably doing the job the packaging implies.'],
